the earlier helper bytes, not this strengthened helper.

## Unbuilt actual SDK fixture

`project/main/main.c` uses real public config init/defaults, certificate-bundle
attach, SSL setup/session reset/free and real heap/adaptive-input APIs. It
observes the new RX allocator's public return values through a fixture-only
wrapper; it does not read private SSL context fields, replace process-wide
allocator dispatchers, or alter TLS content sizes. Ordinary wrapper calls
forward to the real SDK functions.

Planned execution covers unlabelled first-client exclusion, occupied public
user-data retention, two independent eligible configs, repeated cleanup/reset
and reopen, dual-core claim/handover, erase-before-reloan and heap integrity.
The fixture's main/worker stacks are isolated test resources, not changes to
production board profiles. No radio/TFT/audio service or network executes.

`project/main/disabled.c` supplies two distinct actual reserve-off controls.
CMake selects this entry instead of main.c, compiles the original SDK ssl_tls.c,
omits the reserve module/static storage and all four RX/heap observation wraps,
and calls the original certificate-bundle attach directly. SDK calloc/free
dispatchers remain ordinary; only the existing canonical reclaim wrapper is
selected when that independent option is on. The disabled smoke tests use real
setup/reset/free/reopen, full public incoming-payload capacity, simultaneous
independent contexts and heap integrity. They do not claim real-heap OOM or
reclaim-recovery coverage beyond what the enabled fixture separately executes.

Four explicitly labelled **ordinal failure injections** at the underlying heap
calloc seam exercise the first four ordinary SDK setup allocations after the
reserved RX allocation. They return test-controlled NULL and are not evidence
of real heap exhaustion. The original setup/rollback/free bodies execute.

Separate actual-heap-pressure cases call the unchanged allocator under internal
heap exhaustion, observe genuine NULL and recovery, and use the whole canonical
reclaim module and adaptive queue. A busy reserve falls back through the SDK
dispatcher; IDLE extras may be released while READY/READING/WRITING leases and
payloads are checked. Injection is disabled for those cases. Target results
must demonstrate the real observed NULL/request/recovery sequence before any
actual allocator claim; none is yet claimed here. Successful ordinary allocations
are forwarded to their original free function, and any reserved pointer reaching
ordinary heap free is a named fixture failure before heap corruption.

## Frozen variants and next commands after review

`freeze-run.py` writes a fresh sibling ignored campaign, canonical candidate,
complete project, actual canonical reclaim/adaptive sources and production
Kconfig snapshot, with all source identities fixed before build. Exact mutations
are recorded separately for marker omission, busy exclusion, setup rollback,
phase-scoped early FREE publication, ordinary free misuse and reclaim bypass.
Expected controls require one exact `TLS_RX_TARGET_FAILURE <name>` plus
`TLS_RX_TARGET_CONTROLLED_STOP`; generic panic/assert, build failure and timeout
must remain nonpass. The early-publication mutant is intentionally phase scoped
to the erase-barrier scenario.

After root review, prepare one fresh campaign per variant:

```text
python -B .build/cyd-tls-reserve-target-prep-02/freeze-run.py --output .build/cyd-tls-reserve-target-01 --variant canonical
```

Its frozen `sources/sdk-build.ps1` builds only that isolated campaign, with
installed SDK/tool paths supplied explicitly. Use `-RepositoryDirectory` for
this worktree and the campaign's frozen launcher. `-DisableReclaim` and
`-DisableReserve` create independent option controls; each must use a fresh
campaign, not overwrite a
previous build. No download, flashing, serial, OTA or reset command is present.
The launcher is not yet executed or target-qualified.

`run-target.py` now supplies the runnable source→build→ELF→compiled-header
closure→merge→QEMU→exact-result chain. It uses the reviewed early-MPI runner's
source/tool/SDK discipline and distinct reserve result logic. It freezes each
variant before creating the parent report; pins the clean SDK and original
inputs; captures installed tools/ROM, actual ELF machine94, exact module and
generated SDK-TU rows, initialized allocator-dispatch words and ownership call
destinations, compiled header closure before/after runtime, config values and
flash merge bytes. Child PID and authoritative terminal exit/log hashes are
saved; timeout terminates the same child tree and remains nonpass. QEMU runs
with no NIC. No device command exists.

Concrete commands **after root review**:

```text
python -B .build/cyd-tls-reserve-target-prep-02/run-target.py --output .build/cyd-tls-reserve-target-compile-01 --variants canonical --compile-only
python -B .build/cyd-tls-reserve-target-prep-02/run-target.py --output .build/cyd-tls-reserve-target-runtime-01
```

The second command defaults to all ten variants: canonical reserve-on/reclaim-on,
six exact mutants, reserve-on/reclaim-off, reserve-off/reclaim-on and
reserve-off/reclaim-off. The four option combinations require real target builds
and runs; the earlier configure-only off case is not their qualification.
The runner checks exact source rows, absence of module/static-slot/RX-wrapper
symbols, actual link wrap options and ordinary callback/allocator routes for
reserve-off builds. SDK/tools/QEMU/PowerShell paths can be supplied
explicitly with their named options. Compile-only leaves `passed=false` and
runtime unrun. Selected runtime variants have their actual narrower scope
reported; `full_target_matrix_complete` requires all ten. `--preflight-only`
creates no output; `--freeze-only` checks the source/tool/SDK preparation path
without SDK export, configure, compilation or QEMU. Both preparation modes have
been exercised only as preparation; no target result is yet qualified.

Production integration, whole-service callback/redirect/Stop/cancel binding,
